# Agent Core Rules

You are an AI coding assistant working on the Motely project, a .NET and Vue-based application for JAML (JSON-Augmented Markup Language) processing and visualization.

## Repository Context

### Primary Technologies

- Backend: .NET 10.0 (C#)
- Frontend: Vue 3 + Vite
- Testing: xUnit (C#)
- Build: dotnet CLI, npm/vite

### Key Directories

- `Motely/`: Core library (.NET class library)
- `Motely.API/`: API layer (.NET class library)
- `Motely.TUI/`: Terminal UI application (.NET console)
- `Motely.Tests/`: Test suite (.NET class library)
- `vue-jaml-ui/`: Vue 3 frontend application
- `wwwroot/`: Static web assets and additional frontends
- `JamlFilters/`: JAML filter definitions
- `SearchResults/`: JAML search result files
- `scripts/`: Build and utility scripts

### Build Commands

- .NET: `dotnet build` (solution-level) or `dotnet build <project>.csproj`
- .NET Tests: `dotnet test`
- Vue UI: `cd vue-jaml-ui && npm run build`

## Workflow Orchestration

All workflows are coordinated through `@agent-orchestrator.mdc`, which routes tasks to stateless helper rules. The orchestrator analyzes user intent and dispatches to appropriate helpers (features, bugfix, testing, lint, etc.).

Helper rules are stateless, cacheable, and designed for parallel execution when beneficial.

## Shared vs Personal Rules

This `.cursor/rules/` directory contains shared team rules committed to git. These rules should:

- Be cross-platform compatible (macOS, Linux, Windows)
- Avoid personal preferences or absolute paths
- Focus on repo-specific conventions and workflows

Personal Cursor preferences should be kept in:

- User-specific Cursor settings (not committed)
- Personal `.cursorrules` file in home directory (if supported)
- Local git-ignored configuration

## Communication Guidelines

- Provide direct answers first; context second if necessary
- Keep status updates to 1-3 sentences
- Use bullets sparingly for key results only
- Avoid narration ("Perfect! Great! Let me...")

## Tool Usage

### Do

- Use cross-platform commands (dotnet, npm, git)
- Check tool availability before use
- Batch multiple edits to the same file when possible
- Ask for approval before risky operations (push, PR, rebase, deploy)

### Don't

- Don't use platform-specific commands without checking availability
- Don't modify external systems without explicit approval
- Don't assume tools are installed without verification
